Rich Payne wrote:

>
> Yes, because after all the engineering directly get the cash from the sale
> I'm sure.

You have a point here of course. But to insist that sales are totally
irrelevant seems a bit overkill to me, that's all.

>
> Yes, but how many SXs do you think they have sitting in Compaq? and you
> think that just because you ask a question on this list, Jay or somebody
> else should run around Compaq getting the hardware together to try it out
> for you? Give me a break.

No, I did not imply that, of course. I was very surprised that with a given
amount of 21164s around nobody cared to check this issue, that's all.
512MB stick didn't come out yesterday.

>
> Well, my "average office" is API NetWorks....the other company that builds
> and sells Alphas.

I know. If the applications you're dealing with do not need that much of memory,
you're in luck, trust me. All vendors seem to bypass 2-4CPU/8-16GB
configurations altogether, and your statement just confirms it once again.
This leaves many folks doing physics-related computations behind. I'm not
the only one around doing this kind of work, believe me.

> >
> > To make clear, I definitely wasn't talking about a secretarisse's office.
>
> Again you need to think about the frame when the SX was designed/tested.

Several folks stated that 21164 is too old. I do see their point.
However, everybody knows price of a single 21264. This will keep
21164s as a primary home Alpha around for some years to come,
I believe, especially giving any lack of prospects of affordable 21264s.

If I understood it correctly, Jay hinted that the required work can be
done, it requires playing around with SROM code. If there is anyone
capable to do this work, I'd happily lend the sticks. Sorry I can't pay
for this work from personal money.

