Hi folks...

-----Original Message-----
From: Adrie van den Berg [mailto:zoekt@vinden.nl]

Harware: Digital aplha server 1000A 5/300
Jumpstart version : 1.1
Linux: Redhet version 7.0
Bios version : 5.70

I'm trying to install Redhet Linux 7.0 on this machine, when i start the 
jumpstart cd ( boot dka400 -fl 0) it boots the cd and starts loading driver 
etc, but after detecting a scsi cd rom drive the system stops.
Does jump start work on an "old" 1000A 5/300 system?

As far as I know, yes. Sadly all the AS 1000 machines we have here seem to
have gone to Alpha Heaven, so it hasn't been tested in a while. Do you see
the "Alpha JumpStart! 1.1 booting" message before it locks up? If so, what
is the last message you get before it hangs? What kind of SCSI controller is
in the system?

> -----Original Message-----
> From:	KirkE@paccessglobal.com [SMTP:KirkE@paccessglobal.com]
> 
> It's possible
> that the JumpStart makes assumptions about what SCSI controller your
> CD-ROM
> should be on or what SCSI IDs you should have set by default.  As the
> CD-ROM
> does a good amount of auto detection for your hardware, I'm still at a
> loss
> to explain what appears to be fairly common behavior.  If anyone within
> Compaq's development team for Jumpstart is on the list, perhaps they can
> shed some light on this.  I will send a query directly with my errors and
> post to the list.
> 
> --KE
> 
Yes, this is correct. The problem is that Linux and SRM use different
methods for numbering devices. SRM thinks of the devices in terms of bus and
hose, but Linux remaps each hose sequentially to a bus in /proc/pci. In
addition, each Alpha system has its own quirks about how it numbers devices.
PWS (Miata) machines will remap IDE devices to SCSI if os_type is set to
UNIX. Machines earlier than EV6 ignore non-PCI buses when assigning PCI bus
numbers, but EV6 and later machines will assign a non-PCI bus a PCI bus
number, and then skip that bus number on all the hoses on the system. 

So to figure out what device is attached to what bus and what hose,
JumpStart has to do a lot of guesswork. And since we no longer have any
functioning AS/1000 machines, it would come as no surprise to me to find out
that JumpStart was guessing wrong on something and getting all mixed up.
However, this should not cause the machine to lock up.

